WEBVTT

00:00:00.998 --> 00:00:07.238
Welcome to vNAAATS. This is the Oceanic Trainee introduction, the area, the controller pack, and natTrak.

00:00:09.382 --> 00:00:15.462
Please note this is a demonstration using sample flights. It is not a live version of the FDD.

00:00:18.694 --> 00:00:23.622
Congratulations on passing your exam. You have taken the first step towards your OCA endorsement.

00:00:23.942 --> 00:00:26.598
Next, one of our instructors will take you onto the scopes.

00:00:26.918 --> 00:00:36.038
Before that, this short video covers what you need, the Oceanic area you will control, the vNAAATS controller pack, and the natTrak website used to process Oceanic clearance requests.

00:00:36.678 --> 00:00:50.310
Before your session, make sure you have the latest stable vNAAATS and EuroScope pack linked from the training page. You also need a Hoppie ACARS code which is free from the Hoppie website so that you can use CPDLC. And you will sign into natTrak with your normal VATSOM details.

00:00:50.598 --> 00:00:57.286
On the day of your live training session, your instructor will issue you a temporary key in order to access the FDD and control the ocean.

00:00:58.022 --> 00:01:05.670
Now the call signs. You can control as Gander Radio, Shanwick Radio, or NAT_FSS, which acts as both Gander and Shanwick.

00:01:05.926 --> 00:01:12.838
For training and for your OC endorsement, you will control as NATFSS. The other call signs are only used during events.

00:01:13.478 --> 00:01:17.286
The oceanic area you will control is split into two halves at 30 west.

00:01:17.542 --> 00:01:26.822
Gander is to the west of 30 West and Shanwick is to the east. Because you control as both, you call yourself Gander Radio or Shanwick Radio, depending on which side of the line the aircraft is.

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:29.222
The northern boundary is the solid blue line.

00:01:29.606 --> 00:01:34.054
North of it is the Reykjavík Oceanic area controlled by Icelandic controllers.

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:40.358
To the south are two other oceanic areas, Santa Maria to the southeast and New York to the southwest.

00:01:40.774 --> 00:01:43.526
The west is Canada controlled by Moncton Centre.

00:01:43.942 --> 00:01:49.702
To the northeast is the United Kingdom controlled by Scottish and to the east is Ireland controlled by Shannon.

00:01:50.022 --> 00:01:52.198
France and Spain lie to the southeast.

00:01:52.486 --> 00:01:55.206
There are transition areas for both Canada and Ireland.

00:01:55.686 --> 00:01:58.086
Ireland it is a light green vertical line.

00:01:58.502 --> 00:02:03.558
Canada hand the aircraft off to Moncton around 30 miles before the oceanic exit point.

00:02:04.294 --> 00:02:08.198
The pack also draws the current NAT tracks taken from the live track message.

00:02:08.454 --> 00:02:11.014
They are shown dashed and move when the tracks change.

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:15.014
The four supersonic routes are fixed and always stay in the same position.

00:02:15.718 --> 00:02:18.662
Now for vNAAATS, this is the Oceanic Controller Pack.

00:02:18.982 --> 00:02:27.590
It has two parts, a plugin that runs inside your Oscope and manages your flight tags and the flight data display or FDD which runs in a web browser or as a Windows app.

00:02:27.942 --> 00:02:32.710
Together they cover tracking, releasing and transferring aircraft, CPDLC and natTrak.

00:02:32.966 --> 00:02:34.950
Let us take a quick tour of the FDD.

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:39.878
Enter your key and the display opens on your position as soon as you are connected to VATSIM.

00:02:40.294 --> 00:02:43.974
The FDD shows the aircraft that will enter or are inside your area.

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:46.950
You can group them by track and sort them by entry time.

00:02:47.462 --> 00:02:52.934
Panels on the right show the flight plan, the NAT track data for the selected aircraft, and the current tracks.

00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:56.710
Click a call sign to select the flight and open the aircraft menu.

00:02:57.062 --> 00:03:05.734
From here, you can track an aircraft, release it, transfer it, change its flight level, Mach number or SEL-CEL, send a contact me and send a supervisor request.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:09.702
Changes you make here are shared with your EuroScope tags automatically.

00:03:10.406 --> 00:03:13.318
CPDLC is built into the FDD.

00:03:13.702 --> 00:03:18.022
Open the CPDLC window and sign into your station with your Hoppie code.

00:03:18.406 --> 00:03:23.142
Pilots who are close to oceanic airspace log on to you and appear in the connected list.

00:03:23.398 --> 00:03:29.094
Click a call sign to read the conversation. Send level changes, contact messages, or standard messages.

00:03:29.446 --> 00:03:35.782
Pilots can also request oceanic clearances by CPDLC and those requests appear at the top of the window.

00:03:36.486 --> 00:03:39.398
PDLC is optional for pilots on VATSIM.

00:03:40.358 --> 00:03:41.734
The track is also built-in

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:46.374
The pending list shows the aircraft that have filed for an oceanic clearance and need action.

00:03:46.758 --> 00:03:49.062
The cleared list shows the ones you have already cleared.

00:03:49.382 --> 00:03:52.998
Open natTrak takes you straight to the website, ready for you to work through them.

00:03:53.702 --> 00:03:58.502
On the natTrak website, log in with your VATSIM details and open the pending messages.

00:03:58.822 --> 00:04:01.958
Work through the aircraft in time order, clicking view on each one.

00:04:02.598 --> 00:04:09.542
The bottom right, natTrak tells you either that there are no active conflicts detected within 10 minutes or that a conflict is detected.

00:04:09.766 --> 00:04:14.854
It checks for loss of separation against other aircraft on the same track by flight level and speed.

00:04:15.142 --> 00:04:18.726
If there is no conflict, click move to process list and review the next one.

00:04:19.078 --> 00:04:23.878
For a reclearance, choose the same NAT track, add the reason in the free text and transmit the reclearance.

00:04:24.166 --> 00:04:26.246
The FDD updates automatically.

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:30.150
It is important to get clearance requests from pilots in good time.

00:04:30.534 --> 00:04:34.950
Since December 2024, Gander and Shanwick no longer issue an oceanic clearance.

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:42.598
Pilots send a request, called an RCL, 30 to 90 minutes before their entry point, with the highest level they can accept their MAX FL.

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:50.342
Inside 30 minutes it is done by voice. Auto RCL reminds pilots who have not asked, and a red star shows the reminder was sent.

00:04:50.662 --> 00:04:56.902
Not every pilot can use CPDLC and it is not required on VATSIM, so natTrak still works as before.

00:04:57.286 --> 00:05:00.358
This is a change of process for pilots as well as controllers.

00:05:00.678 --> 00:05:03.046
Flights leaving Keflavik need no request.

00:05:03.718 --> 00:05:05.094
That is everything for now.

00:05:05.478 --> 00:05:11.718
The training page has these notes, the downloads, and our how-to videos for the FDD, CPDLC, and natTrak.

00:05:12.038 --> 00:05:14.103
Good luck and we will see you on the scopes

